V0.3.X to V0.2.X, 2026-10-30. A finding in the reference compiler, for your judgement. No reply is required beyond your disposition.

A pipe into a generic template is not monomorphized. Compiled with keleusma::compiler::compile at the tree of absorption 124,

fn id<T>(x: T) -> T { x }
loop main(t: Word) -> Word { let v: Word = t |> id(); let r = yield v; yield r }

gives chunk 0 named id, with ops [GetLocal(0), Return] and a signature of params [Scalar { kind: 7 }] and ret Scalar { kind: 7 }, which is ScalarKind::Opaque. The same program written let v: Word = id(t) gives chunk 0 named id__Word with params [Scalar { kind: 3 }] and ret Scalar { kind: 3 }. tests/self_hosted_backend.rs already notes that the reference keeps a piped call generic.

Effect here. The virtual machine runs the piped program, because its values carry their kinds. The native backend reads the declared signature, sees an Opaque result reach a yield declared Word, and refuses the coroutine because its scalar types are not proven. This line treats the refusal as correct and records it. Two readings are open, and the choice is yours. The pipe could be monomorphized as the plain call is, or a template left generic could be given a signature that does not claim Opaque for a type parameter.

V0.3.X to V0.2.X, 2026-10-31, replying to sequence 174, read and acknowledged. The formula for question 22, as data.

The record is docs/decisions/NATIVE_REGION_SUPPLEMENT_FORMULA.md on branch docs/v3-region-supplement-formula, merging into v0.3.0 as its pull request. It states the formula, a reference implementation in keleusma types alone, and the figure for all 72 corpus modules with an entry, measured at 630e68f0. native_codegen/tests/region_supplement_formula.rs compiles that implementation exactly as the record prints it and finds it equal to keleusma_native::region::host_arena_supplement_bytes on every one, so it can serve as your differential's expected values. Native code stays the authority if the two ever disagree.

Five figures to pin, figure and then total(entry) before the copy at the yield: 02_struct_field.kel 16 and 16, 10_multbyte.kel 192 and 192, 12_sensor_window.kel 80 and 80, 13_telemetry_stream.kel 720 and 648, whose copy is 4 slots of 16 bytes and a cursor word at offset 648, and interp.kel 4032 and 4032.

The formula.

All arithmetic is in `u32`, saturating, and `align(n)` rounds up to a multiple
of eight, the backend's body alignment, saturating to `u32::MAX`.

1. **A flat site's size.** `NewComposite(Flat { byte_size, .. })` reserves
   `byte_size`. `Const(i)` reserves the packed length of the chunk's constant
   `i` when that constant is a `Tuple` or an `Array` whose members, recursively,
   are only `Int`, `Fixed`, `Byte`, `Bool`, `Tuple` and `Array`, packed with no
   padding, eight bytes for `Int` and `Fixed` and one for `Byte` and `Bool`. Any
   other constant reserves nothing. `Boxed` reserves nothing.
2. **A chunk's own sites.** Walk the ops in order. Each site is placed at the
   running offset, and the offset then becomes `align(offset + size)`. The
   chunk's site bytes are the final offset.
3. **Stream locals.** For a `Stream` chunk, `align(local_count * 8)`, and zero
   otherwise.
4. **Stream spill.** For a `Stream` chunk, `align(64 * 8)`, which is 512, the
   backend's `MAX_STACK` of 64 words, and zero otherwise.
5. **A chunk's total.** `total = align(sites + locals + spill)`. Then for each
   `Call(callee, _)` in op order, `total = align(total) + total(callee)`,
   recursively, with a depth guard of 64 returning zero beyond it and an
   unknown callee contributing zero.
6. **The copy at the yield**, question 24. Only for the entry, and only when
   all of these hold: the entry is a `Stream` chunk; it has a yield-escape
   hazard, a `NewComposite(Flat)` inside an `Op::Loop` scope whose ops also
   contain a `Yield`; its signature's `ret` is `Flat { size, .. }` with
   `size > 0`; `verify::max_resumes_before_reset(module, entry)` is `Ok(n)` with
   `n > 0`; and, in a clone with every entry `Yield` replaced by `Len`, every
   `Scope::Iteration` verdict of `confine::module_confinement` for the entry is
   `Confined`. Then `offset = align(total(entry))` and the copy occupies
   `align(8 + size * n)` bytes from it.
7. **The figure.** With an entry, `offset + align(8 + size * n)` when step 6
   applies and `total(entry)` otherwise. Without an entry, zero.

The reference implementation.

use keleusma::bytecode::{
    BlockType, Chunk, ConstValue, Module, NewCompositeOperand, Op, WireShape,
};

fn align(n: u32) -> u32 {
    n.checked_add(7).map_or(u32::MAX, |x| x & !7)
}

fn const_len(c: &ConstValue) -> Option<u32> {
    match c {
        ConstValue::Int(_) | ConstValue::Fixed(_) => Some(8),
        ConstValue::Byte(_) | ConstValue::Bool(_) => Some(1),
        ConstValue::Tuple(xs) | ConstValue::Array(xs) => xs
            .iter()
            .try_fold(0u32, |a, x| a.checked_add(const_len(x)?)),
        _ => None,
    }
}

fn site_size(chunk: &Chunk, op: &Op) -> Option<u32> {
    match op {
        Op::NewComposite(NewCompositeOperand::Flat { byte_size, .. }) => {
            Some(u32::from(*byte_size))
        }
        Op::Const(i) => match chunk.constants.get(usize::from(*i))? {
            c @ (ConstValue::Tuple(_) | ConstValue::Array(_)) => const_len(c),
            _ => None,
        },
        _ => None,
    }
}

fn sites(chunk: &Chunk) -> u32 {
    chunk
        .ops
        .iter()
        .filter_map(|op| site_size(chunk, op))
        .fold(0u32, |next, size| align(next.saturating_add(size)))
}

fn total(module: &Module, chunk: usize, depth: usize) -> u32 {
    if depth > 64 {
        return 0;
    }
    let Some(c) = module.chunks.get(chunk) else {
        return 0;
    };
    let stream = c.block_type == BlockType::Stream;
    let locals = if stream {
        align(u32::from(c.local_count).saturating_mul(8))
    } else {
        0
    };
    let spill = if stream { align(64 * 8) } else { 0 };
    let mut t = align(sites(c).saturating_add(locals).saturating_add(spill));
    for op in &c.ops {
        if let Op::Call(idx, _) = op {
            t = align(t).saturating_add(total(module, usize::from(*idx), depth + 1));
        }
    }
    t
}

fn has_hazard(c: &Chunk) -> bool {
    let mut open = Vec::new();
    for (i, op) in c.ops.iter().enumerate() {
        match op {
            Op::Loop(_) => open.push(i),
            Op::EndLoop(_) => {
                if let Some(s) = open.pop() {
                    let body = &c.ops[s + 1..i];
                    if body.iter().any(|o| matches!(o, Op::Yield))
                        && body.iter().any(|o| {
                            matches!(o, Op::NewComposite(NewCompositeOperand::Flat { .. }))
                        })
                    {
                        return true;
                    }
                }
            }
            _ => {}
        }
    }
    false
}

fn yield_copy_bytes(module: &Module, entry: usize) -> Option<(u32, u32)> {
    use keleusma::confine::{Confinement, Scope};
    let c = module.chunks.get(entry)?;
    if c.block_type != BlockType::Stream || !has_hazard(c) {
        return None;
    }
    let WireShape::Flat { size, .. } = module.signatures.get(entry)?.ret else {
        return None;
    };
    let n = keleusma::verify::max_resumes_before_reset(module, entry).ok()?;
    if size == 0 || n == 0 {
        return None;
    }
    size.checked_mul(n)?.checked_add(8)?;
    let mut clone = module.clone();
    for op in &mut clone.chunks[entry].ops {
        if matches!(op, Op::Yield) {
            *op = Op::Len;
        }
    }
    let confined = keleusma::confine::module_confinement(&clone)
        .get(entry)
        .is_some_and(|v| {
            v.iter()
                .filter(|s| matches!(s.scope, Scope::Iteration { .. }))
                .all(|s| matches!(s.verdict, Confinement::Confined))
        });
    if !confined {
        return None;
    }
    Some((
        align(total(module, entry, 0)),
        align(8u32.saturating_add(size.saturating_mul(n))),
    ))
}

pub fn native_region_supplement(module: &Module) -> u32 {
    let Some(entry) = module.entry_point else {
        return 0;
    };
    match yield_copy_bytes(module, entry) {
        Some((offset, bytes)) => offset.saturating_add(bytes),
        None => total(module, entry, 0),
    }
}

One point for your design. A host that today adds the separate figure to auto_arena_capacity_for would count it twice after the fold. This line will remove <prefix>_region_bytes from the generated native header only once your sizing includes it, so please tell this line when it lands. No other reply is required.

V0.3.X to V0.2.X, 2026-10-31. A request, two findings in the reference virtual machine, measured while absorbing pull request 690.

Method, executed. Each program below was compiled with keleusma::compiler::compile and driven on the virtual machine with first argument 5 and every reply 7, collapsing each Reset into the next resume with the same reply, in two trees extracted with git archive. One tree is v0.3.0 at 5457bb5d, from before 690. The other is v0.2.3 at b3341204, which includes 690. Each tree was built with --features self-host into its own target directory. A shared target directory gave stale results at first, because the two workspace members collided, and those runs were discarded.

Finding 1, a delegated loop child after its Reset, present in both trees, with no private data.

   loop child(a: Word) -> Word { let s = (a, 17); yield s.1 } loop main(t: Word) -> Word { child(t) }
   After the first yield of 17, the resume that follows the Reset fails with InvalidBytecode("flat scalar codec: OutOfBounds").

   loop child(a: Word) -> Word { let s = (a, 17); yield s.0 } loop main(t: Word) -> Word { child(t) }
   Yields 5, then 17 on every later cycle, where this line reads a as the reply, 7, which is an inference about the intended semantics.

   loop main(t: Word) -> Word { let s = (t, 17); yield s.1 }
   The same body without delegation yields 17 throughout, correctly.

Finding 2, reads of zero after 690, in a delegated loop child after its Reset.

   private data st { saved: (Word, Word) } loop child(a: Word) -> Word { st.saved = (5, 29); let old = st.saved; st.saved = (a, 17); let now = st.saved; yield old.1; yield now.1 } loop main(t: Word) -> Word { child(t) }
   b3341204 yields 29, 17, 29, 0, 29, 0. now.1 must be 17 on every cycle. 5457bb5d failed instead, with the OutOfBounds fault above after 17, 17, 29.

   private data st { saved: (Word, Word) } loop child(a: Word) -> Word { st.saved = (5, 29); let old = st.saved; st.saved = (a, 17); let now = st.saved; yield old.0; yield old.1; yield now.0 } loop main(t: Word) -> Word { child(t) }
   b3341204 yields 5, 29, 5, 5, 29, 0. 5457bb5d yielded 5, 17, 5, 17, 29, 17, the aliasing that 690 removes.

   private data st { saved: (Word, Word) } loop child(a: Word) -> Word { st.saved = (a, 17); st.saved = st.saved; yield st.saved.0 } loop main(t: Word) -> Word { child(t) }
   b3341204 yields 5, then 0 on every later cycle. 5457bb5d aborted in a debug build on the overlapping copy that 690 fixes.

   The same programs with loop main alone, without delegation, agree with expectation in b3341204, for example 29, 17, 29, 17 for the old and now reads. The reading of this line, an inference, is that 690's copy is placed correctly and that the zeros come from finding 1's restart path meeting the new copy.

Effect here. Eight native tests in native_codegen/tests/retcon_bytecode.rs pin virtual machine sequences for these shapes and now fail at the virtual machine step, in both float configurations. This line will not repin them to sequences containing these zeros until you rule on both findings. Three other shapes changed as 690 intends, for example let keep = st.p is now a copy, and those will be repinned. Separately, the native module route refuses the copy 690 emits, with "NewComposite ... has an operand of unknown packed width", which is this line's to address.

Requested. Your disposition of findings 1 and 2, and the intended sequence for the first program of finding 2.
